1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Nc3 a6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. Be3 Bb4 7. Bd3 {(There
is a division of opinion concerning this move. Some authorities prefer Be2)}
Nge7 8. O-O Bxc3 9. bxc3 O-O 10. f4 f6 {(This move is of doubtful merit)} 11.
Rf3 d5 12. Rg3 f5 13. e5 Qc7 14. Qh5 Rf7 15. Kh1 ({This appears to be loss of
time.} 15. Nf3 {was the proper line of play and would have been very difficult
to meet}) 15... Nxd4 16. cxd4 b5 17. Rg5 g6 18. g4 Rg7 19. Qh4 Bb7 20. Rg1 Rf8
21. gxf5 Nxf5 22. Bxf5 Rxf5 {(Black here plays very judiciously and selects
the right course to ensure a draw)} 23. Rxf5 gxf5 24. Qf6 Rxg1+ 25. Kxg1 Qg7+
26. Qxg7+ Kxg7 {[Illustrated London News 11 April 1874 and Huddersfield Chess
Magazine, May 1874, give the game as ending here]} 27. a3 Kg6 28. Kg2 Kh5 29.
c3 {Played at City of London CC, City Restaurant, 34 Milk Street, Cheapside,
London. Not less than 700 people watched the event! (including Staunton,
Steinitz, Lowenthal, Horwitz, Zukertort, Bird, Blackburne, etc). 20 games were
played altogether. These seven show just one from each board - source: City of
London CC Magazine, 1874, page 71. Sources: Westminster Papers, 1 Apr 1874;
Illustrated London News, 11 April 1874} 1/2-1/2

