1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. Bc4 Bc5 5. d3 d6 6. O-O Bg4 7. a3 ({There is
no good purpose answered by this move.} 7. Ne2 {in order to play it afterwards,
would have been better}) 7... Nd4 8. b4 Bb6 9. Bg5 Nxf3+ 10. gxf3 Bh3 11. Re1
h6 12. Bh4 (12. Be3 {was the proper line of play. The move in the text
involves the loss of a piece}) 12... g5 13. Bg3 h5 14. Kh1 h4 15. Ne2 hxg3 16.
fxg3 Bf2 17. Qd2 Bxe1 18. Rxe1 Nh5 19. g4 Qf6 20. Qe3 Nf4 21. Rg1 Qh6 22. Qf2
Qh4 23. Qxh4 Rxh4 24. Ng3 Rh7 {Mr. Keynes has played the greater part of this
game in a very skilful manner, but he has been aided materially by his
opponent's want of foresight. And after a few more moves the game was won by
White [sic - presumably they mean Black - JS]} 25. c3 O-O-O 26. d4 Rdh8 27. Ne2
Bxg4 28. Rxg4 Rxh2+ 29. Kg1 Nxe2+ 30. Kf1 Nxc3 31. dxe5 Rb2 32. Ke1 Rh1+ 33.
Bf1 Nb5 34. exd6 Nxd6 35. Rxg5 Rb1+ 36. Ke2 Rbxf1 37. Kd3 Rxf3+ {Source:
Westminster Papers, 1 Apr 1874} 0-1

