1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Bc5 5. c3 dxc3 ({Very weak} 5... Nf6 {
was the correct move, and would have brought the game to a position in the
Giuoco Piano, where neither side has any advantage}) 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qd5+ Ke8
8. Qxc5 d6 9. Qxc3 Nf6 10. O-O Qe7 11. Re1 Ne5 12. Nxe5 dxe5 13. Qb3 c6 14. Bg5
Rf8 15. Nd2 Be6 16. Qg3 h6 17. Bxf6 Qxf6 18. Nf3 Rd8 19. Nxe5 Qf4 {(It is not
usually considered advisable with a pawn behind to try and exchange queens.
Moreover, in other respects, the move in the text is objectionable)} 20. Qg6+
Bf7 21. Nxf7 {(We see no reason why White should not have captured the g-pawn)}
Rxf7 22. Qe6+ Kf8 23. f3 Re8 24. Qc4 Qg5 25. Qd3 a6 26. Rad1 Re6 27. Kh1 Kg8
28. Qd8+ Qxd8 29. Rxd8+ Rf8 30. Rxf8+ Kxf8 31. Rd1 Ke7 32. g3 Rd6 33. Rxd6 Kxd6
34. Kg2 Ke5 35. Kf2 Kd4 36. Ke2 b5 37. Kd2 c5 38. Kc2 a5 39. Kd2 a4 40. Kc2 b4
41. b3 a3 42. Kc1 g5 43. g4 c4 44. bxc4 Kxc4 45. Kc2 b3+ 46. axb3+ Kb4 47. e5
a2 48. Kb2 {Source: Westminster Papers, 1 Apr 1874} 1-0

