1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Bb4+ ({Safer is} 4... Bc5) 5. Bd2 ({
The usual course here is} 5. c3) 5... Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 d6 7. Nxd4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Qf6
9. Qxf6 Nxf6 10. Nc3 O-O 11. O-O Be6 12. Bd3 a6 13. a3 Rae8 14. Rae1 Bd7 15. f4
Bc6 16. Re3 Nd5 {A mistake, which ought to lose the game.} 17. exd5 Rxe3 18.
dxc6 bxc6 19. Bxa6 Rb8 20. Rb1 {This blunder turns the tables.} Rxc3 21. Bd3
Rxa3 22. b3 Rba8 23. Kf2 Ra1 24. Rxa1 Rxa1 25. h3 c5 26. Kf3 d5 27. Ke3 Rc1 28.
g3 c4 {Very injudicious, Black should bring his king into action.} 29. bxc4
dxc4 30. Be4 Ra1 31. Kd4 Ra3 32. c3 Ra1 33. Kxc4 Rg1 34. g4 Rg3 35. Kd4 Rxh3
36. c4 Kf8 37. Kd5 Ke7 38. Kc6 Kd8 39. Kd5 Rh4 40. Ke5 Rxg4 41. Bxh7 g6 42. c5
f6+ 43. Kxf6 Rxf4+ 44. Ke5 g5 45. Bf5 Rf2 46. c6 Rf4 47. Bd7 Rf7 {Adjudicated
win for Black. Source: Westminster Papers, 1 April 1874, p255. The newspaper
places the game alongside other games marked as played in the second round but
in reality, to tally with results as reported, it was more probably played in
the third game between the players.} 0-1

