1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 (4. exd5 {at this point is considered
better}) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Nxf6+ Bxf6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Qe2 (9.
Bd3 {with the view of castling on the king's side, would have been stronger})
9... Nc6 10. O-O-O a5 11. Kb1 Bd7 12. Ne5 Rad8 13. Qe4 {(This move loses a
pawn, without any compensating advantage in position being gained)} Qxf2 14.
Bb5 f5 15. Qd3 Nxe5 16. dxe5 Bxb5 17. Qxb5 Qxg2 18. Qb3 Rxd1+ 19. Rxd1 Qc6 20.
Qd3 b6 21. Qd7 Qxd7 22. Rxd7 Rc8 23. Re7 g6 24. Rxe6 f4 25. Kc1 Rd8 26. Rf6 Rf8
27. Kd2 Rxf6 28. exf6 Kf7 29. Kd3 Kxf6 30. Ke4 g5 31. a4 Ke6 32. c3 Kf6 33. b4
axb4 34. cxb4 Ke6 35. a5 bxa5 36. bxa5 Kd7 37. Kd5 Kc8 38. Kc6 Kb8 39. a6 Ka7 {
won in 43 moves, acc. to the Times report. Game end acc. Westminster Papers.
Source: Westminster Papers, 1 Apr 1874} 0-1

